    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In 2014, an unprecedented number of children fled violence from Honduras, El Salvador, and Guatemala, crossing Mexico’s southern border in search of better opportunities in the United States. On June 2, 2014, a presidential memorandum by Barack Obama described the arrival of Central American minors as an “urgent humanitarian situation,” bringing the matter to public and political attention. Drawing from a content analysis of 50 news articles published by El Universal and Reforma in 2014, this study revealed that the Mexican press’ coverage of Central American minors shifted after the issue garnered political attention. In the latter half of 2014, the Mexican press published a greater number of articles about their arrival, increased the word count of stories about minors, and included other stakeholders in the debate, such as human rights activists. Given the increased presence of human rights representatives in the coverage, the tone toward migrant minors was slightly more positive after June. Articles were also analyzed using the media framing theory. Results showed that the Mexican press focused on those responsible for addressing the situation, such as government officials, and highlighted conflict between different sides of the public debate. In doing so, the press left migrant minors, the main stakeholders affected by these policies and public debates, in the background, as a nameless and voiceless mass. This study, therefore, makes a call for nuanced representations of Central American migrant minors to humanize their stories in the Mexican press.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
